.TH "DHCPT" "1" "October 2026" "dhcpt 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dhcpt \- comprehensive DHCP tester, troubleshooting, and diagnostic CLI
utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]dhcpt\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] [\f[I]INTERFACE\f[R]]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]dhcpt\f[R] \f[B]\-i\f[R] \f[I]INTERFACE\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]dhcpt\f[R] \f[B]\-\-list\-interfaces\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]dhcpt\f[R] \f[B]\-\-list\-options\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]dhcpt\f[R] \f[B]\-\-install\-skill\f[R] [\f[I]TARGET\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]dhcpt\f[R] is a Layer 2 and Layer 3 DHCP testing, diagnostic, and
troubleshooting tool designed for Linux network engineers and system
administrators.
It crafts RFC\-compliant DHCP Discover packets, listens for DHCP Offers,
and thoroughly decodes network parameters, lease lifetimes, Option 82
Relay Agent parameters, and RFC 3442 Classless Static Routes.
.PP
\f[B]dhcpt\f[R] is strictly \f[B]lease\-safe\f[R]: out of the standard
four\-step DORA exchange (Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge), it
only sends a DHCP Discover and inspects incoming DHCP Offers, but never
sends a DHCP Request or allocates an IP address.
.PP
On Layer 2 Ethernet interfaces, \f[B]dhcpt\f[R] uses raw packet sockets
(\f[B]AF_PACKET\f[R]).
On Layer 3 interfaces without hardware MAC addresses (e.g., WireGuard
\f[B]wg0\f[R], OpenVPN \f[B]tun0\f[R]), \f[B]dhcpt\f[R] automatically
switches to raw IP sockets (\f[B]AF_INET\f[R]).
Both modes require superuser privileges (\f[B]sudo\f[R]) because opening
raw network sockets in the Linux kernel requires the
\f[B]CAP_NET_RAW\f[R] capability.
.PP
On Layer 3 interfaces, Layer 2 broadcast is not supported; dedicated
DHCP servers must be specified via \f[B]\-\-dhcp\-servers\f[R] (or
\f[B]\-s\f[R]).
Because packet transmission on Layer 3 relies on kernel network routing,
the Linux routing table must direct traffic for the target DHCP server
IP(s) out through the specified Layer 3 interface.
\f[B]dhcpt\f[R] automatically checks kernel route egress and issues an
operational warning if a target server is routed via a different
interface.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Interface Targeting
.TP
\f[I]interface\f[R], \f[B]\-i\f[R] \f[I]INTERFACE\f[R], \f[B]\-\-interface\f[R] \f[I]INTERFACE\f[R]
Target network interface to send DHCP Discovers on (e.g.
\f[CR]eth0\f[R], \f[CR]ens3\f[R], \f[CR]bond0\f[R], \f[CR]wg0\f[R]).
May be passed as an option flag or as a single positional argument.
Use this option to bind packet capture and transmission to a specific
network device.
.SS DHCP Relay & IP\-Helper Simulation
.TP
\f[B]\-\-dhcp\-servers\f[R] \f[I]DHCP_SERVERS\f[R], \f[B]\-\-dhcp\-server\f[R] \f[I]DHCP_SERVERS\f[R], \f[B]\-s\f[R] \f[I]DHCP_SERVERS\f[R]
Target one or more remote DHCP servers directly via unicast
(comma\-separated, e.g.
\f[CR]\-\-dhcp\-servers 10.1.1.1,10.1.1.2\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-s 10.1.1.1\f[R]).
Corresponds to the relay target configured on routers and switches (e.g.
Cisco \f[I]ip helper\-address\f[R]).
When querying remote servers, \f[B]dhcpt\f[R] automatically populates
the BOOTP relay agent gateway (\f[I]giaddr\f[R]) with the local
interface IP address so the server routes the reply directly back to the
tester.
Use this option to verify whether remote central DHCP servers are
reachable and listening on UDP port 67 across routed networks.
Aliases: \f[B]\-\-dhcp\-server\f[R], \f[B]\-s\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-target\-gateway\f[R] \f[I]GATEWAY_IP\f[R], \f[B]\-\-relay\-subnet\f[R] \f[I]GATEWAY_IP\f[R]
Simulate originating from a remote subnet by specifying the gateway IP
defined for that pool in the DHCP server configuration (RFC 3527 Link
Selection, Option 82 Sub\-option 5).
Instructs the DHCP server which address pool to allocate from, while
\f[B]dhcpt\f[R] sets the BOOTP relay gateway (\f[I]giaddr\f[R]) to the
local interface IP address so the DHCP Offer reply is routed back to the
tester.
Use this option to remotely verify whether a central DHCP server has an
active, non\-exhausted address pool for a specific VLAN or subnet
without having physical access to that network segment.
Typically used in combination with \f[B]\-\-dhcp\-servers\f[R] (or
\f[B]\-s\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-circuit\-id\f[R] \f[I]CIRCUIT_ID\f[R]
Simulate the Option 82 Agent Circuit ID sub\-option per RFC 3046 (e.g.
\f[CR]Vlan100\f[R], \f[CR]ge\-0/0/1\f[R]).
Use this option to verify VLAN\-specific or port\-specific DHCP
allocation policies.
Many enterprise DHCP servers (such as Kea or ISC DHCP) use the Circuit
ID to assign specific IP ranges, boot files, or option sets to
particular VLANs.
If clients on a specific VLAN fail to obtain an IP or receive incorrect
parameters, pass their VLAN tag (e.g.
\f[CR]\-\-circuit\-id Vlan100\f[R]).
Typically used in combination with \f[B]\-\-dhcp\-servers\f[R] and
\f[B]\-\-target\-gateway\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-remote\-id\f[R] \f[I]REMOTE_ID\f[R]
Simulate the Option 82 Agent Remote ID sub\-option per RFC 3046 (e.g.
switch hostname, MAC address, or DUID).
Use this option to test switch\-specific access control lists or
location\-based allocation policies.
In secure enterprise networks, DHCP servers may reject requests or
allocate distinct pools based on which physical switch forwarded the
request.
Typically used in combination with \f[B]\-\-dhcp\-servers\f[R] and
\f[B]\-\-target\-gateway\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-giaddr\f[R] \f[I]GIADDR\f[R]
Explicitly override the BOOTP Relay Agent Gateway IP address
(\f[I]giaddr\f[R]).
By default, \f[B]dhcpt\f[R] automatically populates \f[I]giaddr\f[R]
with the IP address of the local interface used to transmit the request,
ensuring the DHCP server routes unicast Offer replies back to the
tester.
Use this option to diagnose legacy DHCP servers that lack RFC 3527 Link
Selection support (which require \f[I]giaddr\f[R] to match the subnet
gateway directly), or when testing multi\-homed routers and VRF routing
topologies.
Typically used in combination with \f[B]\-\-dhcp\-servers\f[R] (or
\f[B]\-s\f[R]).
.SS Protocol & DHCP Packet Options
.TP
\f[B]\-o\f[R] \f[I]DHCP_OPTIONS\f[R], \f[B]\-\-request\-options\f[R] \f[I]DHCP_OPTIONS\f[R]
Comma\-separated list of additional DHCP option codes (e.g.
\f[CR]12,26,66,67\f[R]) or symbolic names (e.g.
\f[CR]hostname,tftp_server_name\f[R]) to append to the Parameter Request
List (Option 55).
Any numeric code between 1 and 254 is accepted.
Standard network parameters (Subnet Mask, Router, DNS, NTP, Domain
Search, Classless Static Routes) are requested by default.
Use this option to verify special provisioning services that rely on
specific DHCP options, such as PXE/netboot installation servers (Option
66 TFTP server, Option 67 bootfile name), VoIP telephone setups (Option
43 vendor info), or MTU discovery (Option 26).
See \f[B]\-\-list\-options\f[R] to view locally supported options and
symbolic names, or consult the official IANA registry: \c
.UR https://www.iana.org/assignments/bootp-dhcp-parameters
.UE \c
\&.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-clear\-default\-options\f[R]
Clear the default requested RFC options list.
Requests only the options explicitly supplied with
\f[B]\-o\f[R]/\f[B]\-\-request\-options\f[R].
Use this option to simulate minimal embedded devices, IoT hardware, or
bare\-metal PXE ROMs that only request a minimal subset of options and
might misbehave when offered large option payloads.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-no\-broadcast\f[R]
Do not set the BOOTP broadcast flag, requesting the DHCP server to send
unicast Offers.
Use this option to test whether the DHCP server correctly supports
unicast delivery to clients that do not accept broadcast traffic.
.TP
\f[B]\-m\f[R] \f[I]MAC\f[R], \f[B]\-\-mac\f[R] \f[I]MAC\f[R]
Override the client hardware MAC address (format:
\f[CR]aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff\f[R]).
Use this option to test static DHCP reservations, MAC filtering rules,
or captive portal bypasses without modifying the physical NIC.
.SS Timing, Detection & Output Format
.TP
\f[B]\-t\f[R] \f[I]TIMEOUT\f[R], \f[B]\-\-timeout\f[R] \f[I]TIMEOUT\f[R]
Timeout in seconds to wait for DHCP Offers (default: \f[CR]5.0\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-a\f[R], \f[B]\-\-all\f[R]
Listen for the full timeout duration to capture all answering DHCP
servers on the segment.
Use this option to detect rogue, misconfigured, or duplicate DHCP
servers operating on the local broadcast domain.
.TP
\f[B]\-j\f[R], \f[B]\-\-json\f[R]
Output results as formatted JSON.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Increase logging verbosity.
Can be specified multiple times (e.g.
\f[B]\-v\f[R] for informational progress messages, \f[B]\-vv\f[R] for
full debug logging).
.TP
\f[B]\-d\f[R], \f[B]\-\-debug\f[R]
Enable detailed debug logging (frame assembly, socket operations, ARP
resolution, and binary option decoding).
Shorthand alias for \f[B]\-vv\f[R].
.SS Utilities & AI Integration
.TP
\f[B]\-l\f[R], \f[B]\-\-list\-interfaces\f[R]
List available local network interfaces, operational states
(\f[CR]operstate\f[R], \f[CR]carrier\f[R]), IP addresses, and MAC
addresses, then exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-list\-options\f[R]
Display all supported RFC DHCP options and their default request status,
then exit.
For the complete global registry of assigned options, see \c
.UR https://www.iana.org/assignments/bootp-dhcp-parameters
.UE \c
\&.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-install\-skill\f[R] [\f[I]TARGET\f[R]]
Install the bundled AI agent skill for Gemini CLI, Claude Code, or
Mistral Vibe.
Supported targets are \f[CR]gemini\f[R], \f[CR]claude\f[R],
\f[CR]mistral\f[R], or \f[CR]all\f[R] (default).
When \f[CR]all\f[R] is selected, \f[B]dhcpt\f[R] automatically detects
which AI assistants are present on the system (by inspecting
configuration directories \f[CR]\(ti/.gemini\f[R],
\f[CR]\(ti/.claude\f[R], \f[CR]\(ti/.vibe\f[R] or CLI binaries in
\f[CR]PATH\f[R]) and only deploys to active environments.
If a skill file or symlink already exists at the destination,
\f[B]dhcpt\f[R] refuses to overwrite it unless \f[B]\-\-force\f[R] is
explicitly specified.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-force\f[R]
Force overwrite of existing skill files or symlinks during
\f[B]\-\-install\-skill\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-completion\f[R] {\f[I]zsh\f[R],\f[I]bash\f[R]}
Generate shell completion script for Zsh or Bash to standard output,
then exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Print version number and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Show command\-line usage summary and exit.
.SH DHCP RELAY & LAYER 3 SIMULATION
When diagnosing DHCP issues across switches and routers, standard
broadcast testing fails because broadcasts do not cross Layer 3
boundaries.
.PP
\f[B]dhcpt\f[R] simulates a network relay agent (e.g.
Cisco \f[I]ip helper\-address\f[R]) by:
.IP "1." 3
Sending a unicast UDP packet from source port 67 to destination port 67
(standard relay forwarding per RFC 2131) to the remote DHCP server
(\f[B]\-\-dhcp\-servers\f[R] / \f[B]\-s\f[R]).
.IP "2." 3
Setting \f[I]giaddr\f[R] to the tester\(aqs machine IP so the DHCP
server routes the reply back.
.IP "3." 3
Injecting RFC 3527 Link Selection (Option 82 Sub\-option 5) with the
target gateway IP (\f[B]\-\-target\-gateway\f[R]) to instruct the DHCP
server to allocate an address from that specific subnet/VLAN pool.
.PP
This allows network administrators to test remote subnets and VLANs from
anywhere with Layer 3 connectivity (including over corporate VPNs and
WireGuard tunnels) without physical access to the target broadcast
domain.
.PP
For detailed packet flow diagrams, vendor configuration directives (e.g.
Cisco, Juniper, Arista, Linux), and RFC 3527 routing mechanics, see the
online guide at: \c
.UR https://github.com/epicade/dhcpt/blob/main/docs/relay-mechanics.md
.UE \c
\ (or locally installed under
\f[I]/usr/share/doc/dhcpt/relay\-mechanics.md\f[R]).
.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
0
Success: all queried DHCP servers replied, or at least one Offer was
captured on broadcast.
.TP
1
Failure / Timeout: zero DHCP Offers received, or permission denied.
.TP
2
CLI usage / syntax error: missing interface, invalid IP/MAC, or unknown
option.
.TP
3
Partial response: multi\-server query (\f[B]\-s\f[R]) where some servers
answered but at least one timed out.
.SH EXAMPLES
Standard local broadcast check:
.IP
.EX
sudo dhcpt \-i eth0
.EE
.PP
Detect rogue DHCP servers on local network:
.IP
.EX
sudo dhcpt \-i eth0 \-\-all \-\-timeout 5
.EE
.PP
Simulate DHCP relay (e.g.
Cisco ip helper\-address) for a remote VLAN using Option 82:
.IP
.EX
sudo dhcpt \-i eth0 \-\-dhcp\-servers 192.0.2.1 \-\-target\-gateway 10.50.1.1 \-\-circuit\-id Vlan100
.EE
.PP
Automatically detect the outgoing VPN tunnel interface when querying a
remote server from a workstation:
.IP
.EX
IFACE=$(ip route get 10.1.1.1 \f[B]|\f[R] grep \-oP \(aqdev \(rsK\(rsS+\(aq)
sudo dhcpt \-i \(dq$IFACE\(dq \-\-dhcp\-servers 10.1.1.1 \-\-target\-gateway 10.50.1.1
.EE
.PP
Test across Layer 3 interfaces without MAC addresses (WireGuard or
OpenVPN TUN):
.IP
.EX
sudo dhcpt \-i wg0 \-\-dhcp\-servers 10.1.1.1 \-\-target\-gateway 10.50.1.1
.EE
.PP
Verify static reservation by spoofing client MAC:
.IP
.EX
sudo dhcpt \-i eth0 \-m 00:11:22:33:44:55
.EE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
If \f[B]dhcpt\f[R] times out without receiving DHCP Offers, use the
following steps to isolate the issue:
.SS 1. Verify UDP Port 67 Reachability (Netcat)
When testing remote DHCP servers (especially across VPN tunnels or
routed firewalls), test if UDP port 67 is accessible:
.IP
.EX
nc \-z \-v \-u \-w 2 <server_ip> 67
.EE
.PP
Options: \f[B]\-u\f[R] (UDP mode), \f[B]\-z\f[R] (zero\-I/O port scan),
\f[B]\-v\f[R] (verbose output), \f[B]\-w 2\f[R] (2\-second timeout).
Note that standard Linux/OpenBSD Netcat does not support GNU\-style long
options.
.PP
If Netcat reports \f[I]Connection to  67 port [udp/bootps]
succeeded!\f[R] but \f[B]dhcpt\f[R] times out, UDP traffic is permitted.
The server may be dropping the query due to pool exhaustion,
unconfigured subnets, or missing Option 82 policies.
.SS 2. Verify Interface Link State
Ensure the network interface is up and has carrier signal:
.IP
.EX
ip link show <interface>
.EE
.SS 3. Inspect Detailed Packet Trees
Run \f[B]dhcpt\f[R] with \f[B]\-vv\f[R] to view outgoing and incoming
packet trees and Option 82 payloads:
.IP
.EX
sudo dhcpt \-i <interface> \-s <server_ip> \-vv
.EE
